            <div xml:id="d266">

                <pb facs="0401" n="319" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg319"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">815.24/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Honduras</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dennis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-01-21T09:00:00-06:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 21,
                                1925—9 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 2:52 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>12. Department’s telegram 6, January 17, 4 p.m. has been communicated to the
                    Provisional President who has decided that for considerations of economy he
                    prefers to purchase the bulk of the arms from the Government of the United
                    States rather than from private companies. He intimated that delivery can be
                    obtained from the United States Government within a month.</p>
                <p>The Provisional President has received telegram from Monico Zelaya, acting
                    consul, New York City, stating that the customhouse authorities there were
                    ignorant of lifting of the embargo. I advised the Provisional President to
                    inform his agents in the United States of the Department’s telegram above
                    mentioned.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dennis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
